    <body>
      <sec id="article-29576.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Strongyloidiasis is a neglected tropical disease caused primarily by <italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides stercoralis</italic>, a soil-transmitted helminth capable of establishing chronic infection through autoinfection. This unique lifecycle allows persistence for decades within a host, often without symptoms. Clinical manifestations range from asymptomatic eosinophilia to gastrointestinal, pulmonary, and dermatologic findings. Severe complications, including hyperinfection syndrome and disseminated disease, may occur, particularly in immunocompromised individuals, and can be life-threatening. Infection also occurs in animals such as dogs, cats, and primates, contributing to transmission dynamics. High clinical suspicion remains essential for timely diagnosis and management.</p>
        <p>This activity enhances participant understanding of the pathogenesis, clinical presentation, diagnostic evaluation, and management of strongyloidiasis. Emphasis is placed on recognizing risk factors, identifying subtle presentations, and initiating appropriate antiparasitic therapy to prevent progression to severe disease. The role of an interprofessional team&#x02014;including infectious disease specialists, pharmacists, nurses, and laboratory professionals&#x02014;is highlighted as essential for accurate diagnosis, medication management, monitoring, and patient education. Collaborative care improves early detection, reduces complications, and supports optimal, evidence-based patient outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify&#x000a0;the life cycle and mechanism of infection of <italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides stercoralis</italic>.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess and evaluate&#x000a0;patients with clinical presentations&#x000a0;compatible with strongyloidiasis.&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine&#x000a0;the&#x000a0;appropriate evidence-based therapy for&#x000a0;patients infected with <italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides stercoralis.</italic></p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply effective strategies to improve care coordination among interprofessional healthcare team members to facilitate positive outcomes for patients&#x000a0;infected with <italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides stercoralis</italic>.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=29576&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=29576">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Strongyloidiasis is primarily caused by the roundworm&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides&#x000a0;stercoralis,</italic>&#x000a0;an intestinal, soil-transmitted helminth belonging to the phylum <italic toggle="yes">Nematoda</italic> (roundworms).<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;S</italic><italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;stercoralis</italic>&#x000a0;mainly infects humans but&#x000a0;can also occur naturally in domestic dogs, cats, and primates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Although strongyloidiasis is generally found in&#x000a0;tropical and subtropical regions, small pockets of transmission can occur in any setting where poor sanitation and hygiene permit fecal contamination of the environment, in rural areas, and among socioeconomically disadvantaged groups.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides stercoralis</italic>&#x000a0;is&#x000a0;prevalent worldwide, particularly in Asia, Africa, South and Central America, and the Pacific. The global burden of infection is likely underestimated owing to limited surveillance, diagnostic challenges, and inadequate epidemiological data in endemic settings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides stercoralis </italic>is&#x000a0;one of the most overlooked parasitic infections among the "neglected tropical diseases".<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides</italic> is important among human nematodes because it can&#x000a0;complete a free-living cycle, including a soil&#x000a0;phase, and can reproduce and amplify within the human host. Rhabditiform larvae excreted in human feces can mature into free-living adult worms in the external environment, where they reproduce and produce infective filariform larvae capable of penetrating intact human skin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Infection with <italic toggle="yes">S stercoralis is associated with a broad clinical spectrum, ranging from asymptomatic infection to symptomatic acute disease, chronic infection maintained by autoinfection, and the hyperinfection syndrome, a life-threatening disseminated strongyloidiasis associated with immunosuppressed patients and with</italic>&#x000a0;high fatality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Diagnosis is based on the demonstration of larvae in clinical specimens, with serology and nucleic acid&#x02013;based assays providing supportive evidence. However, diagnostic methods are not fully standardized, and performance varies by technique, specimen type, and parasite burden, contributing to missed or delayed diagnoses. Ivermectin remains the treatment of choice due to its high efficacy and tolerability. Longer or repeated courses may be needed in patients with hyperinfection or disseminated disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;In recent years, strongyloidiasis has increasingly been recognized as a global public health concern. This disease has been incorporated into the World Health Organization's control strategy for soil-transmitted helminth infections, which emphasizes the need for reliable epidemiological data to accurately define the global burden of disease and access to effective treatment to support prevention, control, and eventual elimination efforts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The genus <italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides</italic> is classified within the order <italic toggle="yes">Rhabditida</italic>, a group that consists largely of free-living, soil-transmitted helminths (STH) nematodes that feed on bacteria and organic matter. STH are intestinal parasites that complete part of their life cycle in soil; more than 50 species of <italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides</italic> have been identified, but most do not infect humans and are associated with animal hosts. Among the species known to infect humans,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S stercoralis</italic> is responsible for the vast majority of strongyloidiasis cases worldwide and is uniquely capable of causing chronic infection through autoinfection, which can progress to life-threatening hyperinfection, usually in immunosuppressed hosts,&#x000a0;and is associated with&#x000a0;a high mortality.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">S stercoralis&#x000a0;</italic>is unique among human helminths because it can complete its life cycle both within the human host and the external environment. Humans are infected transcutaneously.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r10">[10]</xref> After initial infection, adult parasitic females reside in the small intestine, where they reproduce by parthenogenesis, producing larvae that are passed&#x000a0;from the stool into the environment. These larvae, excreted in human feces, mature into free-living adult worms in soil, where they reproduce and produce infective filariform larvae. Under suitable environmental conditions, these larvae can develop into infective forms capable of penetrating human skin, thereby perpetuating transmission.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Strongyloidiasis is a neglected tropical disease caused predominantly by the&#x000a0;STH&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S stercoralis</italic>. The infection is most prevalent in Southeast Asia and the Western Pacific, where climatic and socioeconomic conditions favor transmission.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Transmission occurs when filariform larvae penetrate intact human skin and migrate to the small intestine, where adult female worms lay eggs that hatch into rhabditiform larvae, which are then shed in stool.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Although skin penetration is the primary route of infection, alternative modes of transmission have been described. Fecal&#x02013;oral and anal&#x02013;oral transmission can occur in settings with poor sanitation or certain sexual practices.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r12">[12]</xref> In addition, transmission through organ transplantation from an infected donor has been documented, highlighting the importance of screening donors and recipients from endemic areas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Infective larvae of <italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides stercoralis</italic> are typically found in moist, fecally contaminated soil, in areas of poor sanitation, where they can survive under favorable environmental conditions. Walking barefoot and occupational exposure to contaminated soil, such as in agricultural work, are well-recognized risk factors for infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Environmental and behavioral factors further contribute to transmission. Poor household sanitation, inadequate sewage disposal, and practices such as walking barefoot facilitate contact with contaminated soil and are consistently associated with higher infection rates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Epidemiologic studies have noted a slight male predominance in strongyloidiasis, which is generally attributed to differential exposure rather than inherent biological susceptibility.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r14">[14]</xref> In many endemic regions, men are more frequently engaged in occupations such as farming, fieldwork, and mining, which involve direct contact with contaminated soil and increase the likelihood of skin exposure to infective filariform larvae. Having a home latrine was associated with a lower risk than using shared latrines.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In developed countries, strongyloidiasis tends to occur primarily in specific risk groups, notably individuals with occupational exposure to soil, such as farmers and miners, who are at increased risk because infective filariform larvae can penetrate intact skin. Cases are also reported among immigrants, refugees, tourists, and military personnel returning from endemic regions. In the United States, the highest prevalence has been documented in immigrants from Africa and Asia; results from one study of Sudanese refugees reported infection rates as high as 46%, followed by lower but still significant rates among individuals from Central and Latin America.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Although<italic toggle="yes"> S stercoralis</italic> is prevalent worldwide, its&#x000a0;true burden is likely substantially underestimated and underreported, making it difficult to plan interventions to control and eliminate the disease in endemic countries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r10">[10]</xref> The limited understanding of the global burden of strongyloidiasis stems from multiple factors. Acute strongyloidosiasis&#x000a0;can be subclinical, or its clinical manifestations may be misdiagnosed or missed entirely. Acute strongyloidiasis can be oligosymptomatic, but may present only with dermatologic manifestations, such as&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">larva currens&#x000a0;(creeping infection), </italic>which is a form of&#x000a0;cutaneous<italic toggle="yes"> larva migrans,&#x000a0;</italic>or with pulmonary symptoms similar to Loeffler syndrome, such as asthma, pulmonary infiltrates, and eosinophilia, or other&#x000a0;symptoms, eg, urticaria, abdominal pain, and diarrhea.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref> Thus, this may mimic many other syndromes, be missed, and progress to a chronic disease.</p>
        <p>Chronic infection with clinical manifestations similar to those of acute disease may be underdiagnosed or misdiagnosed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients can have autoinfection and can develop into a disseminated hyperinfection syndrome many years after initial exposure, mostly in immunosuppressed patients, which can result in high mortality. The underestimation of the burden of strongyloidiasis is further hampered by&#x000a0;diagnostic challenges, including the low sensitivity of conventional stool-based tests, underreporting in high-incidence settings, and weak surveillance systems in endemic regions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Recent systematic reviews, using different methodologies, have attempted to estimate the global prevalence of disease. Older data demonstrated that&#x000a0;strongyloidiasis&#x000a0;had&#x000a0;infected 370 million people globally, with a higher burden in tropical and subtropical regions, where 10% to 40% of the population&#x000a0;can be affected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;A systematic review by Fleitas&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">et&#x000a0;</italic><italic toggle="yes">al</italic>&#x000a0;in 2020 used data on the prevalence and burden of hookworm infections as a proxy to address diagnostic challenges and estimate the burden of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S</italic>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">stercoralis</italic>&#x000a0;infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;They estimated the global burden of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S</italic>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">stercoralis</italic>&#x000a0;infections to be 386 million people.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A systematic review published by Duonfrate&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">et al</italic>&#x000a0;in 2020, using prevalence data from endemic countries from 1990 to 2016, estimated that the prevalence of strongyloidiasis was 8.1%, corresponding to roughly 614 million people infected with&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S stercoralis</italic>&#x000a0;worldwide, with the highest burden in sub-Saharan Africa, Latin America, and East Asia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;A 2021 systematic review found the highest prevalence in the Western Pacific region, affecting 9% to 21% of the population, and the highest seroprevalence in the Eastern Mediterranean, with up to 40% of the population affected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;A distinctive feature of <italic toggle="yes">S stercoralis</italic> is its ability to cause autoinfection, in which larvae develop into infective forms within the host and reinvade via the intestinal mucosa or perianal skin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r11">[11]</xref> This process enables the parasite to persist, with chronic infections reported&#x000a0;for decades&#x000a0;in the absence of treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Host-related factors play an important role in both susceptibility to infection and progression to severe disease. Human T-lymphotropic virus type 1 infection is one of the most well-established risk factors, strongly associated with treatment failure, hyperinfection syndrome, and disseminated disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0; Other conditions linked to increased risk include diabetes mellitus, chronic alcoholism, and immunosuppression. Among these, the use of immunosuppressive therapies, particularly corticosteroids, represents the most significant risk factor for severe strongyloidiasis, as steroids&#x000a0;not only suppress host immune responses&#x000a0;but may also directly enhance larval proliferation. Although HIV infection is frequently cited as a risk factor for strongyloidiasis, current evidence suggests that it does not substantially increase the risk of hyperinfection or disseminated disease. This observation may reflect differences in immune control mechanisms, as HIV-related CD4 T-cell depletion predisposes primarily to bacterial and viral opportunistic infections, whereas protective immunity against helminths involves complex Th2-mediated and eosinophil-driven responses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Life Cycle of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides stercoralis&#x000a0;</italic></bold>
</p>
        <p>A key feature of this parasite is its ability to cause autoinfection, allowing infection to persist for decades. This organism's life cycle includes both free-living and parasitic phases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;(See <bold>Image.</bold> Life Cycle of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides stercoralis</italic>.)&#x000a0;In the free-living phase, rhabditiform larvae passed in stool either develop into infective filariform larvae or mature into free-living adults that reproduce sexually, thereby producing a new generation of filariform larvae. In the parasitic phase, filariform larvae penetrate intact skin and migrate via the bloodstream or lymphatics to the small intestine. There, they mature into adult females that reproduce by parthenogenesis, generating rhabditiform larvae. These larvae are either excreted in feces or transform into filariform larvae within the host, resulting in autoinfection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Parasitic phase</bold>
</p>
        <p>Infection occurs through skin contact with soil containing infective filariform larvae. After penetrating the skin, the larvae migrate via the bloodstream to the lungs, ascend the trachea, and are swallowed. In the small intestine, they invade the proximal bowel mucosa, where they mature into adult females.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r19">[19]</xref> Adult worms can persist in the intestinal mucosa for years, producing eggs that hatch<italic toggle="yes"> in situ</italic>, releasing&#x000a0;rhabditiform (noninfective) larvae. These larvae migrate to the intestinal lumen and are passed in the feces. In the environment, rhabditiform larvae may develop directly into infective filariform larvae or enter a free-living cycle, in which male and female worms reproduce sexually, producing larvae that subsequently become infective.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Autoinfection</bold>
</p>
        <p>Some rhabditiform larvae mature within the intestinal lumen into infective filariform larvae. These larvae can penetrate the intestinal mucosa or perianal skin, initiating an internal autoinfection cycle that allows the parasite to persist within the host.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;An intact immune response generally limits autoinfection. However, even in an immunocompetent host, low-level autoinfection can occur and, subsequently, cause chronic infections for decades, and may only lead to clinical manifestations when superimposed immunosuppression is present.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyperinfection</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hyperinfection syndrome is the most severe manifestation of the disease and is associated with a high mortality rate. This results from an intensified autoinfective cycle, triggered either by newly acquired infection or reactivation of latent, previously asymptomatic strongyloidiasis&#x000a0;in immunosuppressed individuals. Hyperinfection most commonly occurs in chronically infected individuals who become immunosuppressed, but it may also develop in patients who acquire infection while immunosuppressed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;During hyperinfection, larvae disseminate widely to the bloodstream, lungs, central nervous system, and other organs. Mucosal disruption and bacterial carriage on migrating larvae&#x000a0;can result in&#x000a0;polymicrobial bacteremia and meningitis. Sepsis due to bacterial translocation from the intestinal wall is a frequent and life-threatening complication.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>In most cases, Strongyloidiasis is asymptomatic, especially in immunocompetent individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Unexplained peripheral eosinophilia may be the only finding, and some studies even report nonspecific symptoms in more than 50% of the affected patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Acute Infection</bold>
</p>
        <p>Early infection may present with an intensely pruritic, serpiginous rash at the site of larval penetration. When filariform larvae in fecal-contaminated soil enter the body through skin penetration, intense pruritus, congestion, and edema are noted at the portal of entry.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;This reaction, known as ground itch, is usually present on the feet or hands but can also occur perianally, abdominally, or virtually anywhere on the body. This intense urticaria can&#x000a0;persist for up to 3 weeks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Rapid intradermal migration of larvae produces <italic toggle="yes">larva currens</italic>, a pathognomonic manifestation characterized by raised, erythematous, serpiginous tracks that may advance at 5 to 15 cm/h. This finding may also occur during autoinfection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>Pulmonary migration of larvae can cause cough and a L&#x000f6;ffler-like syndrome with dyspnea, wheezing, eosinophilia, and transient pulmonary infiltrates. Larval passage through the pulmonary circulation may result in alveolar hemorrhage and eosinophilic pneumonitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Gastrointestinal manifestations include abdominal pain, diarrhea, vomiting, and epigastric discomfort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Chronic Infection</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides stercoralis</italic> infection may persist for years through ongoing low-level autoinfection. Chronic infection is often mild and may present with intermittent cough, wheezing, dyspnea, abdominal pain, anorexia, diarrhea, or constipation, which can result in inflammatory pneumonitis and pulmonary hemorrhage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;Dermatologic findings, such as urticaria,&#x000a0;may recur.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyperinfection</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hyperinfection results from an accelerated autoinfection, leading to a marked increase in larval burden and&#x000a0;direct tissue damage by the larvae.&#x000a0;Clinical features reflect intensified larval migration; disseminated disease may occur, with larvae detected in organs beyond the usual pulmonary&#x02013;intestinal cycle, including the liver, pancreas, kidneys, mesenteric lymph nodes, brain, and skeletal muscle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r12">[12]</xref> Patients commonly present with fever, sepsis, and unexplained gram-negative or polymicrobial bacteremia resulting from bacterial translocation from the intestine, facilitated by larval penetration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Pulmonary manifestations include cough, dyspnea, wheezing, hemoptysis, diffuse infiltrates, and, in severe cases, respiratory failure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref> Gastrointestinal symptoms may include abdominal pain, diarrhea, ileus, nausea, vomiting, and gastrointestinal bleeding. Larval dissemination beyond the usual pulmonary&#x02013;intestinal cycle can lead to central nervous system involvement, hepatobiliary and renal complications, and characteristic periumbilical purpura.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Eosinophilia is often absent despite severe infection, usually due to steroid therapy before the hyperinfection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Evaluation for <italic toggle="yes">S stercoralis</italic> infection should be guided by epidemiologic risk, clinical presentation, and host immune status. A high index of suspicion is required, particularly in individuals from endemic areas, those with unexplained eosinophilia, or patients who are immunosuppressed or about to receive immunosuppressive therapy.&#x000a0;Screening is recommended before initiating corticosteroids, biologic agents, or other immunosuppressive therapies in at-risk individuals, as early detection and treatment can prevent hyperinfection syndrome and its associated high mortality.</p>
        <p>The diagnosis of <italic toggle="yes">S stercoralis</italic> infection is challenging because larval excretion is often low and intermittent, resulting in low sensitivity of stool microscopy and low specificity of serology. No single gold-standard test or universally accepted diagnostic reference method exists.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r27">[27]</xref> In practice, stool examination, in combination with serologic testing and molecular methods, is recommended, particularly in patients with compatible epidemiologic exposure or unexplained eosinophilia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Peripheral eosinophilia is common in chronic infection but lacks sensitivity and may be absent in severe disease. Notably, eosinophilia is often absent in hyperinfection syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Demonstration of larvae in stool remains the definitive diagnostic method. However, the sensitivity of a single direct stool examination is poor, estimated at approximately 21%. Concentration techniques improve detection rates: the Baermann method reaches about 72% sensitivity, and agar plate culture up to 89%, although infection may still be missed even with repeated sampling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;Molecular assays such as polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and reverse transcriptase-PCR demonstrate very high sensitivity and nearly 100% specificity, but are not widely available and remain limited to specialized laboratories.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Serologic testing, including indirect fluorescent antibody test, enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA), and NIE antigen&#x02013;based luciferase immunoprecipitation system, is considered the most sensitive and is frequently used for screening and for excluding infection due to its high negative predictive value.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;Despite this, negative serologic results do not completely exclude infection. When clinical suspicion remains high, clinicians should pursue stool examination and consider repeat or paired serologic testing. Positive results should be interpreted cautiously in patients, however, as specificity is limited by possible cross-reactivity with other helminths, particularly filarial infections or prior exposure,&#x000a0;although this limitation appears less significant with newer-generation assays.&#x000a0;ELISA-based coproantigen detection may help identify active infection, but stool-based methods alone are insufficient to rule out disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In hyperinfection syndrome and disseminated strongyloidiasis, the high parasite burden makes detection of <italic toggle="yes">S stercoralis</italic> larvae more reliable. Larval migration through the lungs allows identification in respiratory specimens, including sputum, bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, bronchial washings and brushings, and lung biopsy samples. The presence of larvae in respiratory specimens indicates active autoinfection and systemic dissemination and should prompt urgent treatment, as delayed recognition is associated with high mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r34">[34]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Treatment is indicated for both symptomatic and asymptomatic individuals, regardless of immune status, with the goal of complete parasite eradication.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Uncomplicated strongyloidiasis is treated with the following agent options:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Ivermectin (200 &#x003bc;g/kg/day for&#x000a0;2 days)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Thiabendazole (25 mg/kg/day for&#x000a0;3 days)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Albendazole (400 mg twice a day for 3 to 7 days)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r35">[35]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Response to treatment should be confirmed with serial stool testing and/or serologic testing for&#x000a0;1 to 2 years after treatment in all patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyperinfection syndrome or disseminated strongyloidiasi</bold>
<bold>s requires:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ivermectin:</bold>&#x000a0;This is administered daily for at least&#x000a0;2 weeks<italic toggle="yes">, </italic>or until the stool is negative for&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S&#x000a0;stercoralis</italic>&#x000a0;larvae for&#x000a0;2 consecutive weeks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Broad-spectrum antibiotics:</bold>&#x000a0;These are used to cover enteric gram-negative organisms, given the risk of bacteremia and sepsis from intestinal bacterial translocation.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>In immunocompromised individuals, therapy may need to be prolonged. Reduction or discontinuation of immunosuppressive therapy should be considered when feasible. Treatment is generally continued until larvae are undetectable in stool, urine, and sputum for at least 14 days.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Special consideration:</bold> Patients from <italic toggle="yes">Loa loa</italic>&#x02013;endemic regions in Africa should be screened for loiasis microfilaremia before ivermectin therapy, as treatment in heavily infected individuals can precipitate severe encephalopathy. Of the agents outlined above, ivermectin is the first-line therapy due to superior efficacy and tolerability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r37">[37]</xref> Albendazole is considered second-line because of lower cure rates, while thiabendazole has largely fallen out of favor owing to frequent adverse events despite comparable efficacy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r37">[37]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>
<bold>Pulmonary Manifestations (L&#x000f6;ffler-Like Syndrome)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Transient pulmonary symptoms with eosinophilia may resemble:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Bronchial asthma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ascariasis (<italic toggle="yes">Ascaris lumbricoides</italic>)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hookworm infection (<italic toggle="yes">Ancylostoma duodenale</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">Necator americanus</italic>)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Drug-induced eosinophilic pneumonia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pneumonia or pneumonitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Acute respiratory distress syndrome&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Gastrointestinal Manifestations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Chronic abdominal symptoms and diarrhea may mimic:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Giardiasis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Amebiasis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inflammatory bowel disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Irritable bowel syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Celiac disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Acute appendicitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intestinal obstruction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Proctitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Peritonitis</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Dermatologic Manifestations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Conditions to consider in patients with pruritic or serpiginous rashes include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Cutaneous larva migrans (<italic toggle="yes">Ancylostoma</italic> spp)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scabies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Allergic contact dermatitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Urticaria of other etiologies</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>With appropriate antiparasitic therapy, the prognosis of acute and chronic strongyloidiasis is generally favorable, particularly in immunocompetent individuals. Chronic infection may persist for years or decades due to autoinfection, but it is usually curable once diagnosed and treated. In contrast, the prognosis&#x000a0;worsens markedly in patients who develop hyperinfection syndrome or disseminated strongyloidiasis, which are conditions&#x000a0;that are often triggered by immunosuppression, especially with corticosteroids. Despite treatment, reported mortality rates in these severe forms remain extremely high, ranging from approximately 70% to nearly 100%, largely due to overwhelming parasite burden, multiorgan involvement, and secondary bacterial sepsis. These outcomes underscore the importance of early recognition and systematic screening in individuals at risk before the initiation of immunosuppressive therapies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r40">[40]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>The&#x000a0;<bold>Table.</bold>&#x000a0;Major Complications of Strongyloidiasis, Underlying Mechanisms, and Clinical Significance outlines the major complications of strongyloidiasis that are associated with larval migration and tissue invasion, as well as those resulting from autoinfection and hyperinfection syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-29576.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table.&#x000a0;Major Complications of Strongyloidiasis, Underlying Mechanisms, and Clinical Significance&#x000a0;</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px; height: 1px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="width: 144px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Category</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 244px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Complication</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 222px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Underlying mechanism</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 293px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Clinical significance</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="width: 144px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Gastrointestinal</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 244px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Chronic diarrhea, abdominal pain, malabsorption</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 222px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Persistent intestinal parasitism and mucosal inflammation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 293px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Weight loss, protein-losing enteropathy, and nutritional deficiencies</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="width: 144px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Gastrointestinal</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 244px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ileus, bowel obstruction, gastrointestinal bleeding, perforation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 222px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Heavy larval burden and transmural invasion</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 293px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Associated with high morbidity; urgent intervention</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="width: 144px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Pulmonary</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 244px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cough, wheezing, pulmonary infiltrates, eosinophilia (Loeffler-like syndrome)</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 222px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Larval migration through the lung parenchyma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 293px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Often transient, but may mimic asthma or pneumonia</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="width: 144px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Pulmonary</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 244px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Pneumonitis,&#x000a0;acute respiratory distress syndrome, respiratory failure</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 222px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Massive larval migration during hyperinfection</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 293px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Major contributor to mortality</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="width: 144px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Hyperinfection syndrome</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 244px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Accelerated autoinfection with increased larval burden</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 222px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Triggered by immunosuppression, particularly corticosteroid exposure</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 293px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Medical emergency with rapid clinical deterioration</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="width: 144px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Disseminated disease</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 244px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Central nervous system, hepatic, renal, and cutaneous involvement</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 222px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Larval spread beyond the pulmonary&#x02013;intestinal cycle</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 293px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Indicates severe disease with very high mortality</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 61px;">
                <td style="width: 144px; height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Infectious (usually hyperinfection syndrome-related)</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 244px; height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Gram-negative bacteremia and sepsis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 222px; height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Translocation of enteric bacteria by migrating larvae</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 293px; height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Common cause of death</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="width: 144px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Infectious&#x000a0;</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 244px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Bacterial meningitis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 222px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Larval carriage of gut flora to the central nervous system</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 293px; height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Often fatal without prompt recognition and treatment</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48.7143px;">
                <td style="width: 144px; height: 48.7143px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Immunosuppression-related</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 244px; height: 48.7143px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Severe disease after steroids or transplantation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 222px; height: 48.7143px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Loss of immune control over autoinfection</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 293px; height: 48.7143px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Can occur even after short courses of corticosteroids</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Strongyloidiasis has been a neglected tropical disease, largely due to diagnostic challenges and a lack of robust epidemiological data.&#x000a0;The World Health Organization has recently published guidelines focusing on&#x000a0;public health control and the elimination of strongyloidosis in high-risk and endemic areas, as well as for specific high-risk populations and individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;As with other STHs, strategies for prevention and control include regular deworming through mass drug administration, water, sanitation, and hygiene (WASH), and health education to sustain the benefits of&#x000a0;the therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;However, in settings with inadequate WASH, rapid reinfection limits its long-term impact, making chemotherapy alone insufficient for sustained control.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r2">[2]</xref> Integrated approaches that include WASH improvements, health education, and behavioral change are essential to reduce environmental exposure, interrupt transmission, and achieve elimination goals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Prevention in High-Risk Individuals</bold>
</p>
        <p>Prevention of chronic and disseminated strongyloidiasis should be considered&#x000a0;for high-risk populations. Screening of asymptomatic patients, especially those with epidemiological risk factors (eg, exposure to endemic areas), particularly before receiving therapies that would render them immunosuppressed. Early identification&#x000a0;and treatment of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S stercoralis</italic>&#x000a0;in at-risk populations help&#x000a0;prevent hyperinfection and disseminated disease.</p>
        <p>Serologic testing in asymptomatic individuals is warranted in the following groups of patients:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients with prior exposure to endemic areas that now require immunosuppressive therapies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Organ donors with relevant environmental or geographic exposure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Military personnel with remote or past service in endemic regions</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Immigrants and refugees from endemic areas&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r44">[44]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>The diagnosis and management of strongyloidiasis require a high index of clinical suspicion and close interprofessional coordination to ensure early detection, appropriate treatment, and eradication of infection. Clinicians are responsible for recognizing risk factors, initiating therapy, and arranging follow-up to confirm parasitologic clearance. Pharmacists play an important role in promoting medication adherence and verifying appropriate dosing to maximize the likelihood of cure with the initial treatment course.</p>
        <p>Nurses contribute by educating patients, monitoring for adverse effects, documenting treatment response, and reinforcing the importance of completing therapy despite minimal or absent symptoms. All patients require follow-up to confirm eradication, typically with serial stool examinations or anti-<italic toggle="yes">Strongyloides</italic> serology for 1 to 2 years. Effective patient counseling and coordinated follow-up by the healthcare team are essential to maintain adherence and reduce the risk of relapse, hyperinfection, and disease transmission.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29576.r36">[36]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29576.s14">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
